{"id": "EUVD-2026-82258", "enisaUuid": "c02228d6-18ef-3781-9684-7561c1dd5dc5", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\ncgroup/cpuset: Make nr_deadline_tasks an atomic_t\n\nThe nr_deadline_tasks variable in the cpuset structure was introduced by\ncommit 6c24849f5515 (\"sched/cpuset: Keep track of SCHED_DEADLINE task\nin cpusets\"). It is reported by sashiko [1] that nr_deadline_tasks\ncan currently be modified by inc_dl_tasks_cs() under rq->lock and\nby cpuset_attach() under cpuset_mutex. So if both updates happen\nsimultaneously, the nr_deadline_tasks variable can be corrupted leading\nto incorrect operations down the road.\n\nFix that by changing its type to atomic_t so that nr_deadline_tasks\nare always atomically updated. This fix patch is a low hanging fruit.\nIt can handle some of the races between a concurrent sched_setscheduler()\nand cpuset_can_attach()/cpuset_attach() calls, but not all of them like\nthe other issue raised by sashiko [2]. This will be handled hopefully\nin a future follow up patch.\n\n[1] https://sashiko.dev/#/patchset/20260626181923.133658-1-longman%40redhat.com\n[2] https://sashiko.dev/#/patchset/20260630033344.352702-1-longman%40redhat.com", "datePublished": "2026-09-17T16:11:46", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-21T12:52:05", "baseScore": 0.0, "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/642648fa7595241e4d491add5007bc33f11defb5", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/e422b32e6a57269ca8164c60674a78bee85cf5fb", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/e5965ae3f7c918151b87e5c925b7978afbeaf638", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/78d9daf5e13ad1448b7dfebac949936710ec7565", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/95220e1f18f6321008f021abc7d6f581f64bcb82"], "aliases": ["GHSA-rm22-rvmg-2466", "CVE-2026-93150"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.2, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "1d2e6cfb-ecd5-3caf-bd0f-f829cadf80c3", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "43d8cbfefa7c59bd3b5fe184535eca476d45304e"}, {"id": "280a97f8-5dfe-326a-a26a-b5e02508cc67", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "5.15.129 <5.16"}, {"id": "28726873-083d-326c-b741-bc2fa9cb928e", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "6c24849f5515e4966d94fa5279bdff4acf2e9489 <e5965ae3f7c918151b87e5c925b7978afbeaf638"}, {"id": "2bff5c28-f8c2-3c58-8a43-40f83cc427e4", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "6c24849f5515e4966d94fa5279bdff4acf2e9489 <95220e1f18f6321008f021abc7d6f581f64bcb82"}, {"id": "32fadb5a-55a7-36e1-b6f1-eb1d4aed0f36", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.18.52"}, {"id": "377918b3-69fd-332d-8b65-3a094857e585", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.6.160"}, {"id": "46444690-1509-37a0-ab86-fc78c6093ec1", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.2.6"}, {"id": "47e4587e-4a98-3c75-9def-b0a2b86a2625", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "6c24849f5515e4966d94fa5279bdff4acf2e9489 <78d9daf5e13ad1448b7dfebac949936710ec7565"}, {"id": "5c9c4e70-3d6b-31fe-85ad-eb4560f26e88", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "6.4.13 <6.5"}, {"id": "79a9bfb4-191d-3f67-bc1d-63c7536d5c2e", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "74fac5bb0d375d6b8f5e7997925063c509089c9c"}, {"id": "84b91b67-7f64-3ef7-aeae-2ce4a2202b66", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "a620fc79-1683-3d6b-affc-6351ea3f85e0", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.3"}, {"id": "ada0bdad-24c3-361c-a0e9-66129c1b2f93", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "6c24849f5515e4966d94fa5279bdff4acf2e9489 <642648fa7595241e4d491add5007